---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
import line from "../data/line.json";

---
<Base title="Home">
  <h1>The Falco Archive</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    An evidence-first archive of the <strong>Falco</strong> family of <strong>Arienzo</strong> in the
    province of Caserta — and of the Valle Caudina, the province of Benevento, and Brisbane.
  </p>

  <blockquote>
    <em>Falco</em> is the Latin for falcon. It became a surname twice over: as a Lombard personal name,
    handed down as a patronymic, and as a nickname for the quick, the sharp-eyed, or the falconer.
    It was therefore coined independently, many times, in many places.
    <strong>There is no single founding Falco family.</strong> The work of this archive is to establish
    which Falcos are actually connected, and to say plainly where the evidence stops.
  </blockquote>

  <h2>What is established</h2>
  <div class="grid two">
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Nine generations, every one documented</h3>
      <p>From <strong>Matteo Falco</strong> and <strong>Francesca Crisci</strong>, whose children were
      baptised at Arienzo from 1767, down to the present. The head of the line is no longer a guess.
      <a href={u("/direct-line")}>Walk the line →</a></p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Pasquale's baptism, found</h3>
      <p>24 October 1767, in the Collegiate Church of Sant'Andrea: <em>infantem natum ex Mattheo Falco,
      ed Francisca Crisci … qui nominatus est Paschalis</em>. It replaced an ancestor who was never
      there. <a href={u("/method")}>What it displaced →</a></p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Three households, read entry by entry</h3>
      <p>Twenty-six register entries reconstruct the families of Matteo, of Pasquale, and of Vincenzo —
      their children, their baptisms, and the children who did not live.
      <a href={u("/households")}>The households →</a></p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>The bishop next door</h3>
      <p>The church in these registers was the second seat of the bishop of Sant'Agata de' Goti — and
      from 1762 to 1775 that bishop was <strong>Alfonso Maria de' Liguori</strong>, who lived in the
      palace beside it. <a href={u("/liguori")}>What that does and does not mean →</a></p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>One family, one parish, for two centuries</h3>
      <p>Crisci, Morgillo, Martone, Rivetti, de Lucia, Arricale, Maione — every in-law surname of the
      line appears in the same Arienzo books, over and over. The Crisci marry in three times.
      <a href={u("/arienzo")}>The marriage network →</a></p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>And a name rarer than the family's own</h3>
      <p><strong>Zampiello</strong>, which marries in at Arpaia in the 1950s, has about
      <strong>123 bearers in the world</strong> — 37 of them in Italy.
      <a href={u("/name")}>The names →</a></p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>The shape of the journey</h2>
  <p>Arienzo, in the province of Caserta, is the home parish — every generation from Pasquale to Raffaele is
  born, married and buried there. In <strong>1913</strong> a son is registered not at Arienzo but at
  <strong>Arpaia</strong>, four kilometres away and across the provincial line into Benevento. From there the
  family moves to <strong>Forchia</strong>, then <strong>Benevento</strong>, and then to
  <strong>Brisbane</strong>. <a href={u("/places")}>The places →</a></p>

  <p class="small">Nothing here is taken on trust because it is in a family tree. Where a record has been
  seen, it is cited. Where it has not, the claim is marked. See <a href={u("/method")}>Method</a>.</p>
</Base>
